'''BITwiki research question:''' how do we make the path ''read → understand → improve → request → draft → review → canonicalize → maintain'' obvious to a newcomer without reproducing Wikipedia's procedural sprawl?

== Simple ↔ technical localization and accessibility ==

* [https://simple.wikipedia.org/ Simple English Wikipedia]
* [https://simple.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:How_to_write_Simple_English_pages How to write Simple English pages]
* [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Simple_English_Wikipedia Simple English Wikipedia — overview article]
* [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Make_technical_articles_understandable Make technical articles understandable] — current English Wikipedia editing guideline.
* [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plain_English Plain English]
* [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basic_English Basic English]
* [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Simplified_Technical_English Simplified Technical English]
* [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Controlled_natural_language Controlled natural language]

Primary BITwiki lesson: accessibility should not require a second disconnected encyclopedia. Prefer one canonical knowledge object with a plain-language lead/orientation, prerequisites, progressively deeper sections, examples, and technical/reference layers. Separate introductory pages should be exceptional rather than the default.

